# Template: the call commands for the members of mu300voice, and nothing else, as root (systemd images; OpenWrt
# has no sudo). Install with visudo's check:
#   sudo install -m 440 sudoers.mu300voice /etc/sudoers.d/mu300voice && sudo visudo -c
#
# sudoers wildcards match spaces and flags too, so this line is not where the grammar is enforced: the command is.
# mu300-voice-api is mu300-voice in an empty environment (no MU300_* variable - another voice.conf, another AT
# directory - and no PATH of the caller's, whatever this sudo keeps) that accepts only
#   status [--json] | call NUMBER [--json] | answer [ID] [--wait S] [--json] | hangup [ID] [--json] | dtmf DIGITS [--json]
# and refuses everything else with exit 2 before the modem is touched: no --demo, no audio, watch, events, demo,
# tone or echo, no unknown option or extra argument. NUMBER is 3-20 digits with at most a leading +, one of
# CALL_ALLOW in /etc/mu300/voice.conf (none when unset) and never an emergency number, ID is 1-9, DIGITS are
# 0-9 * # A-D; each is checked exactly as it goes into the AT command.
# Never list mu300-voice itself here: that is the administrator's command, with the audio test and the watch.
# Not here, on purpose: mu300-at (the AT channel is the whole modem - radio, SIM, data). Uncomment the SMS line only
# for a service that sends messages ("sms send" takes NUMBER TEXT and goes through the same checks as on the shell).
%mu300voice ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/mu300/bin/mu300-voice-api
# %mu300voice ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/mu300/bin/sms send *
